  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nStrong west winds continue along the western foothills as of noon\nbut peak gusts are starting to slacken as mid level winds are\ndecreasing, in response to exiting upper level trof lifting\nthrough southern SK. Despite temps that are 20-25F cooler than\nWednesday, very dry air is resulting in RH values in the mid teens\nto lower 20s. Please be careful to not spark a fire.\n\nHave dropped the gap locations' wind advisory but for now will \nlet the warnings continue for other zones, with gusts near 60 mph\nstill being observed over our NW as of noon. Gusts east of the \nwarned area are in the 40s to lower 50s. Overall, peak gusts \nshould continue to slowly decrease through the remainder of the \nday. Winds tonight will be lighter but tomorrow will again be \nbreezy/well-mixed under a zonal flow aloft, with gusts on the \norder of 30-40 mph. RHs tomorrow will be similar to today. Winds \nfinally decrease as this regime exits Friday night.\n\nSatellite imagery shows a weak upstream shortwave near the WA \ncoast. This wave will track eastward quickly tonight and will \nbring some increase in mid and high clouds, but likely nothing \nmore as the air mass is quite dry. That said, cannot completely \nrule out sprinkles/flurries/virga over our western mountains. \nRegarding temperatures, look for mid 60s to lower 70s tomorrow and\nmid 60s to mid 70s Saturday, the latter being a little warmer \nover the east as flow backs to the SW ahead of the next trof.\n\nSaturday night through Monday offers potential for a cool & wet\nweather system. Confidence in much cooler temps and higher chances\nof precip (including mountain snow) is high. Details are much more\nuncertain but it should be noted that the GFS has trended toward a\ndeeper trof with a period of upsloping N-NE winds (rather than a\ndrier open wave). Expect snow accumulation above 7kft, which along\nwith much colder temps will stop the ongoing high elevation snow\nmelt and let river flows subside a bit. If a dynamic low develops\nto our south, leaving us in a diffluent region with upslope winds,\nwe could be looking at a MUCH-NEEDED precipitation event that not\nonly includes mountain snow, but potential wet snow along the \nfoothills. A few ensembles even show some wet snow at Billings, \nwhich for the time of year would not be unprecedented (~3\" fell in\nthe city over Memorial Day weekend in 2012). Current indications \nare for 0.20-0.50\" of precip north of the foothills, and \n0.50-1.00\" along the southern foothills and north aspects of our \nsouthern mountains. Also, 90th percentile QPF is for near an inch\nat Billings and 2\" over the southern mountains. Long story short,\nstill a lot to be worked out yet so monitor the forecast.\n\nLook for high temps in the 40s/50s Sunday/Monday, then a trend\nwarmer to the 60s/lower 70s by the middle of the week. Consensus\nis for higher heights toward midweek, but there may be a weaker\nshortwave and shower potential at some point during this time.\n\nJKL",
